 `function-inlining: aggressive`), best of 3 runs. For each program the old tree (commit `4fde78a`: old programs, old stdlib, old compiler) was measured first and the new tree straight after, so slow drift of the machine hits both. Outputs of every row match the C reference.","caveats":"Machine noise is 10–30% between measurements minutes apart (an earlier before/after taken 25 minutes apart showed sieve 39% slower with byte-identical assembly). Back-to-back pairing reduces it but does not remove it; rows inside the grey band are unchanged.\nOnly the three marked n-body programs were edited. For the unedited ones I compared the generated assembly of the old and new compiler on sieve (safe, naive) and graph (safe): identical, so their movement is machine noise; I did not compare the rest.\nThe stdlib chart uses best of 3 per preset, measured one after the other, not the full harness.\n`@unroll` was not used in any program: nearly every hot loop has a variable bound, which the unroller cannot unroll, and unrolling a loop with literal bounds was worth about 2–3% (loop-unrolling aggressive vs off).\nLinux/x86-64 only; Windows and Wine were not measured."}}
